    "text": "The salt air clung to Elias\u2019s skin like a second layer of wool, a constant reminder of the decades he had spent in the iron cage of the lighthouse. At seventy-two, his joints creaked in harmony with the groaning metal of the tower. He was the last of the keepers, a relic in a world of automated sensors and satellite navigation.\n\nOne Tuesday, the mail boat arrived with a single, yellowed envelope. The postmark was faded, but the date was unmistakable: *October 14, 1974*.\n\nElias frowned. He had been the keeper here for thirty years. Before him, it had been his father. Before that, his grandfather. No one had missed a mail run in half a century. The letter was addressed simply to *The Keeper, Blackrock Light*.\n\nHis hands trembled as he broke the wax seal. Inside was a single sheet of paper, the ink brown and brittle.\n\n*Dear Keeper,*\n\n*If you are reading this, the storm has finally broken. I am sorry I could not stay to see the dawn. I left the light burning for you, not for the ships, but for the thing that follows them. Do not look at the water after midnight. It is not the sea that is dark, but the eyes beneath it. Keep the light on. Keep it bright. If the beam falters, they will know you are alone.*\n\n*\u2014 Arthur*\n\nElias dropped the paper. Arthur had been the keeper before his grandfather. Arthur had vanished in 1974 during a hurricane, his body never found. The official report said he had fallen overboard.\n\nA cold dread, colder than the Atlantic, settled in Elias\u2019s stomach. He looked at the clock. It was 11:58 PM.\n\nHe rushed to the lantern room, his heart hammering against his ribs. The great Fresnel lens was dark. The automatic rotation mechanism, which he had checked only an hour ago, had stopped. The bulb was out.\n\nPanic surged. He fumbled for the emergency generator switch, but his fingers were too numb. He slammed his hand against the manual crank, the old backup system. *Clank. Clank. Clank.* The gears ground, resisting.\n\nAt 12:01 AM, the light flickered on, weak and stuttering.\n\nElias leaned against the railing, gasping for air. He looked out at",
